---
title: "JPG to PDF: Turn Photos and Images into One PDF"
layout: base.njk
date: 2026-11-07
updated: 2026-11-07
meta_title: "JPG to PDF Converter: Images to One PDF, A4 or Letter, No Upload"
meta_description: "Turn JPG and PNG images into one PDF in your browser. Put the pages in order, choose A4, US Letter or the image size, portrait or landscape and a margin. Phone photos come out upright. Nothing is uploaded."
meta_keywords: "jpg to pdf, image to pdf, png to pdf, photos to pdf, convert jpg to pdf, combine images into pdf, jpg to pdf without upload"
noIconFont: true
faqs:
  - q: "Are my photos uploaded to a server?"
    a: "No. The PDF is built by your browser with an open-source library called pdf-lib, which runs on this page. Your images never leave your device, which is why the tool works even after you switch off the internet once the page has loaded."
  - q: "Does converting reduce the quality of my photos?"
    a: "Usually not at all. JPG and PNG files are placed into the PDF exactly as they are, without being compressed again. Only photos that need turning upright, and formats a PDF cannot hold such as WebP or GIF, are redrawn and saved as high quality JPEG first."
  - q: "Should I pick A4 or Letter?"
    a: "A4 is the standard paper size in India and most of the world, and it is what government portals, banks and colleges in India expect. US Letter is slightly wider and shorter and is the standard in the United States and Canada. Fit to image makes every page exactly the size of its picture, with no white border unless you add a margin."
  - q: "Why was my phone photo sideways in other converters?"
    a: "Phones often save a photo sideways and add a small tag that tells viewers to turn it. Many converters ignore the tag. This page reads it and turns the picture upright before it goes into the PDF."
  - q: "How do I make the PDF smaller for an upload limit?"
    a: "The PDF is roughly the size of the images inside it. Resize or compress the photos first with the Image Resizer, then convert them here. Choosing fewer pages or cropping out empty space helps too."
  - q: "Can I convert iPhone HEIC photos?"
    a: "Safari can read HEIC, so on an iPhone or a Mac the photos convert directly. Most other browsers cannot open HEIC, and the page tells you which files it could not read. Set the iPhone camera to Most Compatible, or share the photo as JPG, and it will work everywhere."
---

