    <article class="qt-prose qt-section">
        <h2>How to turn images into a PDF</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Choose your images, or drag them from a folder onto the box. You can add more in several goes.</li>
            <li>Put them in order. Each image becomes one page, from the top of the list down. Drag a row to move it, or use the up and down arrows, which also work with a keyboard and on phones.</li>
            <li>Pick the page size, the orientation and the margin. Automatic orientation turns the page sideways for wide pictures and keeps it upright for tall ones.</li>
            <li>Press Create PDF. The file downloads straight away. Open it once to check the pages before you send it.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Which settings to use</h2>
        <p>For documents you will upload to an Indian portal, such as an application form, a KYC submission or a college admission, A4 with a small margin is the safe choice. A4 is the paper size used across India and in most countries, and it prints without anything being cut off. US Letter is the standard in the United States and Canada, so pick it when the PDF will be printed there. Fit to each image makes every page exactly the size of its picture. That suits a set of screenshots, a photo album or a design file, where a white border would only get in the way.</p>
        <p>The margin adds white space around each image. A small margin keeps the edge of a scanned page away from the very edge of the paper, which helps when the PDF is printed, because most printers cannot print right to the edge. Choose no margin when the picture should fill the whole page.</p>

        <h3>Photos that come out sideways elsewhere</h3>
        <p>Phone cameras often store a photo the way the sensor saw it and add a small tag that tells viewers which way is up. Gallery apps read the tag, so the photo looks right on your phone, but many PDF converters ignore it and the page comes out sideways or upside down. This page reads the tag and turns each photo upright before it is placed, so the PDF looks the way the photo looks in your gallery.</p>

        <h3>Image quality and file size</h3>
        <p>JPG and PNG images go into the PDF exactly as they are. They are not squeezed again, so no detail is lost and the PDF ends up close to the combined size of the pictures. Photos that need turning, and formats a PDF cannot hold such as WebP and GIF, are redrawn and stored as high quality JPEG. If a portal limits the upload to a few hundred kilobytes, make the photos smaller first with the <a href="/image-resizer/">Image Resizer</a>, then convert them here. A photo of a printed page usually stays easy to read at a much smaller size than the camera saved it at, so check the text after resizing.</p>

        <h3>Private by design</h3>
        <p>The PDF is assembled on this page by pdf-lib, an open-source library that runs in your browser. Your photos are never uploaded, so ID cards, certificates and bank statements stay on your device. Once the page has loaded, the tool keeps working with the internet switched off.</p>

